From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
Subject: [PATCH] ecm_classifier: do not wait for a reply on multicast

With accel_delay_pkts set to 1, the default and DSCP classifiers deny
acceleration of a UDP flow until they see a packet in the reply
direction. A routed multicast flow never has one, so it stays in
software. Skip the wait when the destination is a multicast group.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
---
--- a/ecm_classifier_default.c
+++ b/ecm_classifier_default.c
@@ -437,9 +437,11 @@ static void ecm_classifier_default_proce
 
 check_delay:
 	/*
-	 * Should we delay the acceleration?
+	 * Should we delay the acceleration? A multicast flow has no return
+	 * direction to wait for.
 	 */
-	if (ecm_classifier_accel_delay_pkts) {
+	if (ecm_classifier_accel_delay_pkts &&
+	    !ecm_ip_addr_is_multicast(ip_hdr->dest_addr)) {
 		if (!ecm_classifier_default_ready_for_accel(cdii, sender)) {
 			DEBUG_INFO("%px: connection is not ready for accel\n", cdii);
 			spin_lock_bh(&ecm_classifier_default_lock);
--- a/ecm_classifier_dscp.c
+++ b/ecm_classifier_dscp.c
@@ -459,8 +459,11 @@ static void ecm_classifier_dscp_process(
 		 * Option 1: Wait forever until to see the reply direction traffic
 		 * Option 2: Wait for seeing N number of packets. If we still don't see reply,
 		 * set the uni-directional values.
+		 * A multicast flow never sees a reply, so it takes the
+		 * uni-directional values at once.
 		 */
-		if (ecm_classifier_accel_delay_pkts) {
+		if (ecm_classifier_accel_delay_pkts &&
+		    !ecm_ip_addr_is_multicast(ip_hdr->dest_addr)) {
 			/*
 			 * Stop the processing if both side packets are already seen.
 			 * Above the process response is already set to allow the
